--- /dev/null
+++ b/HEGEL_REPORT.md
@@ -0,0 +1,155 @@
+# Hegel Property-Based Testing Report — xml-rs (`xml` 1.3.0)
+
+## 1. Summary
+
+Nine hegel property-based tests were added to the existing integration test
+files (`tests/event_reader.rs`, `tests/event_writer.rs`, shared generators in
+`tests/util/pbt.rs`), covering parse robustness, deep-nesting and
+entity-expansion attack inputs, event-stream well-nestedness, well-formedness
+rejection, parser-config consistency, and write→read round-trips at the
+writer's escape-set boundary.
+
+Result: **two real data-loss bugs found in the writer's escaping** (pinned as
+deliberately-failing tests), plus **one quadratic-parse-time performance issue**
+(documented with an `#[ignore]`d reproducer). The reader's billion-laughs
+defenses and deep-nesting behavior check out: entity bombs are rejected with a
+clean `Err`, and 50,000-deep nesting parses without stack overflow.
+
+Setup note: `hegeltest` requires rustc 1.86, above this crate's declared
+`rust-version = "1.73"`, so it was added with
+`cargo add --dev hegeltest --ignore-rust-version`. If these tests are ever
+upstreamed, the dev-dependency MSRV is a maintainer decision to call out.
+
+## 2. Properties Added
+
+In `tests/event_reader.rs` (module `pbt_reader`):
+
+1. **`parser_never_panics_on_arbitrary_input`** — driving `EventReader` to
+   `EndDocument`/`Err` never panics, on arbitrary bytes, arbitrary text, and
+   XML-shaped token soup, under arbitrary combinations of nine `ParserConfig`
+   flags. Also checks the documented terminal-event contract: after
+   `Err`/`EndDocument`, `next()` returns the same event again.
+2. **`event_stream_is_well_nested`** — on both writer-produced valid documents
+   and XML soup, every `EndElement` matches the innermost open `StartElement`
+   (same prefix + local name, LIFO), and `EndDocument` is only emitted with no
+   elements open — for every prefix of the stream, even when parsing later
+   errors.
+3. **`deeply_nested_documents_never_crash`** — sweeps `<a><a>…</a></a>` depth
+   1..=1200 (closed and truncated variants): no stack overflow, exact
+   start/end event counts for closed documents, `Err` (not a panic) for
+   truncated ones. Depth is capped only to protect the test's runtime from
+   the quadratic-parse-time issue below, not any library contract.
+4. **`entity_expansion_bombs_are_rejected`** — generated billion-laughs DTD
+   bombs (fanout 2–10 × nesting levels 1–12; hegel exhausted all 108
+   combinations): whenever the full expansion exceeds the default
+   `max_entity_expansion_length` (1 MB) the parser must return `Err`; it must
+   never deliver more than a small multiple of the limit; and small benign
+   expansions must expand to exactly the expected text. The limits *are*
+   enforced — bombs die in microseconds with `SyntaxError::EntityTooBig`.
+5. **`malformed_documents_are_rejected`** — mismatched close tags, duplicate
+   attributes, names starting with a non-NameStartChar, truncated documents,
+   stray end tags, forbidden control characters, and valueless attributes are
+   all rejected with `Err`, never a panic and never a successful parse.
+6. **`config_variations_agree_on_canonical_stream`** — event-shaping config
+   options (`cdata_to_characters`, `whitespace_to_characters`,
+   `ignore_comments`, `coalesce_characters`, etc.) never change the canonical
+   document content (element structure, attributes, merged text) of a
+   well-formed document. (`trim_whitespace` excluded: it changes text by
+   design.)
+
+In `tests/event_writer.rs` (module `pbt_writer`):
+
+7. **`document_write_read_roundtrip`** (the crown) — a generated document tree
+   (nested elements with Unicode NCNames, attributes, PCDATA and CDATA text at
+   the escape-set boundary, comments) written with `EventWriter` reads back
+   with `EventReader` as exactly the same canonical structure.
+8. **`pcdata_text_write_read_roundtrip`** — arbitrary text (boosted with `<`,
+   `>`, `&`, quotes, `]]>`, entity-reference look-alikes, full Unicode planes)
+   written as `Characters` is recovered exactly.
+9. **`attribute_write_read_roundtrip`** — arbitrary attribute names/values
+   (including quotes, `<`, `&`, `\r`, `\n`) round-trip exactly.
+
+Shared generators live in `tests/util/pbt.rs`: XML-1.0-valid content chars,
+NCNames built from the `NameStartChar`/`NameChar` productions, encode-set
+boundary text, a recursive document-tree generator, an XML-soup generator, and
+canonical-stream parse/compare helpers.
+
+## 3. Bugs Found
+
+All three findings are oracle-independent (round-trips of values the test
+still holds; wall-clock growth), found or confirmed by the properties above,
+and left unfixed per instructions.
+
+1. **`\r` in character data does not survive write→read** (real bug, pinned by
+   the deliberately-failing test
+   `pbt_writer::known_bug_pcdata_carriage_return_lost_in_roundtrip`).
+   `PcDataEscapes` in `src/escape.rs` escapes only `<`, `>`, `&`, so
+   `EventWriter` emits a literal CR; the reader's XML-mandated end-of-line
+   normalization then turns it into `\n`. `Characters("a\rb")` reads back as
+   `"a\nb"`. The writer already escapes `\r` as `&#xD;` in *attribute* values
+   (which round-trip correctly) — PCDATA should get the same treatment. The
+   same loss affects CDATA sections.
+2. **`\t` in attribute values does not survive write→read** (real bug, found
+   by hegel with shrunk counterexample value `"\t"`; pinned by
+   `pbt_writer::known_bug_attribute_tab_lost_in_roundtrip`).
+   `AttributeEscapes` escapes `\n` and `\r` but not `\t`; XML 1.0 §3.3.3
+   attribute-value normalization (implemented by the reader and pinned by the
+   crate's own `test_attribute_normalization`) turns the literal tab into a
+   space, so `attr("a", "x\ty")` reads back as `"x y"`. The writer should emit
+   `&#x9;`.
+3. **Parse time is quadratic in element nesting depth** (performance/DoS
+   issue, documented by the `#[ignore]`d reproducer
+   `pbt_reader::known_issue_deep_nesting_quadratic_time`). Every
+   `StartElement` calls `NamespaceStack::squash()` (`src/reader/parser.rs:714`),
+   which rebuilds the full flattened namespace mapping by walking the whole
+   stack — O(depth) per element, O(depth²) per document. Measured (debug
+   build): depth 1,000 ≈ 27 ms; 10,000 ≈ 2.5 s; 50,000 ≈ 61 s. A ~300 KB
+   hostile document costs a minute of CPU, and the input-size cap the README
+   recommends (`Read::take`) does not defend against it. Not process-aborting
+   (no stack overflow, memory stays bounded), so it is documented rather than
+   pinned as a failing assertion.
+
+Verified non-bugs: entity-expansion limits are enforced (`EntityTooBig` in
+µs for all bomb shapes); no stack overflow at any tested nesting depth; small
+entity chains expand to exactly the right text; duplicate attributes are
+rejected namespace-aware.
+
+## 4. Verification (Run and Reflect)
+
+- **Liveness / mutation check**: three properties were temporarily broken —
+  the PCDATA round-trip assertion inverted, the entity-bomb *guarded* branch
+  assertion inverted (to prove the `expansion > limit` guard actually fires),
+  and the well-nested property's `EndDocument` branch inverted (to prove that
+  branch is reached). All three failed with cleanly shrunk counterexamples
+  (`text = ""`; `fanout = 3, levels = 12` — the minimal >1 MB bomb; a minimal
+  one-element document). Mutations reverted, `.hegel/` database removed.
+- **Exploratory high-count run**: all nine properties were run once at
+  `test_cases = 1000` (10x default) with per-case counters confirming the
+  cases actually ran (1,029–1,309 counted cases per property;
+  `entity_expansion_bombs_are_rejected` ran exactly 108 — hegel exhaustively
+  enumerated its whole 9×12 input space). No new failures. Instrumentation
+  reverted; committed tests use the default count.
+- **Final state**: the full suite (unit tests, xmlconf conformance corpus,
+  streaming, EOL-normalization, version, doc-tests) is green except the two
+  deliberately-failing known-bug pins in `tests/event_writer.rs`.
+
+## 5. Notes and Limitations
+
+- **Generator domain choices** (each documented in code comments):
+  `\r` is excluded from PCDATA/CDATA text generators and `\t` from
+  attribute-value generators, because those inputs are pinned by the two
+  canonical known-bug tests — sibling properties stay green and keep testing
+  everything else. Comment content excludes `-` (XML forbids `--` in comments
+  and the writer intentionally rewrites it, so it cannot round-trip by
+  design). Generated names carry no `:` (namespace prefixes would need
+  bindings) and never equal `xmlns`. Text/content chars are drawn from the
+  XML 1.0 `Char` production — the reader rejects other chars by design, and
+  XML cannot represent them even as character references.
+- The deep-nesting property caps depth at 1200 purely for runtime (the
+  quadratic issue); the `#[ignore]`d reproducer covers larger depths. Run it
+  with `cargo test --test event_reader -- --ignored --nocapture`.
+- `.hegel/` was added to `.gitignore`.
+- Not covered (candidates for future properties): UTF-16/Latin-1 encoding
+  round-trips, namespace-prefixed round-trips (writer prefix rewriting makes
+  the expected output non-trivial), `perform_indent` output re-parsing, and
+  a stateful model test of interleaved `skip()`/`next()`.
